------------+-----------------------------------
          0 |        306       51.69       51.69
          1 |        286       48.31      100.00
------------+-----------------------------------
      Total |        592      100.00

.         
.         * Following the approach outlined in our pre-analysis plan (PAP), we 
> construct a variable to identify which cases are included in the analysis. Ou
> r objective is to examine the parenting effect associated with raising a daug
> hter. We assume that this effect requires sufficient exposure to the parentin
> g experience, meaning that the firstborn daughter must be at least one year o
> ld at the time the gender equality attitudes are measured. To operationalize 
> this, we create a variable named "sample", which takes the value 1 for indivi
> duals meeting this exposure condition. Specifically, we define the year of bi
> rth of the firstborn child—ensuring the respondent has no additional children
> —and compare it to the year the gender attitude questions were answered. Resp
> ondents whose firstborn child was born at least one year prior to the measure
> ment of attitudes are included in the analysis (i.e., sample == 1).
.         
.         gen year_first_born = lb0287_h if (lb0290_h == - 2) & (lb0287_h >= 0)
(34,587 missing values generated)

.     label variable year_first_born "Year of the firstborn when the individual
>  has only one child"

.         
.         * The variable sample is defined as the difference between the year i
> n which the dependent variable (gender equality attitudes) is measured and th
> e year of birth of the firstborn child. We include in the analysis only cases
>  where this difference is at least one year, ensuring that the respondent has
>  been exposed to parenting the firstborn child for a minimum of one year. 
.         
.         gen sample = (syear - year_first_born >= 1) if!missing(year_first_bor
> n)
(34,587 missing values generated)

.         label variable sample "1 if the individual's one child firstborn has 
> at least one year wrt the dep. var.'"
note: label truncated to 80 characters

. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         * Our pre-analysis plan (PAP) proposed using five indicators to const
> ruct a composite measure of gender equality. The idea was to rely on measured
>  attitudes that are plausibly correlated with the latent construct of gender 
> equality. Initially, we intended to use principal component analysis (PCA) to
>  combine the five SOEP variables into a single index. However, upon accessing
>  a random sample of 5% of the data, we found that the indicators were not con
> sistently measured across the same survey years. This lack of temporal overla
> p prevented the use of PCA. As an alternative, we selected the three variable
> s most closely aligned with our conceptual definition of gender equality, whi
> ch emphasizes equality in opportunities, responsibilities, and rights between
>  women and men, and girls and boys. Specifically, we interpret variable plj06
> 20 as capturing attitudes toward gender equality in rights, plj0624 as reflec
> ting beliefs about educational equality (linked to opportunities), and plj062
> 5 as indicating views on shared responsibilities. To construct the composite 
> index, we recoded the three variables so that higher values represent more eg
> alitarian attitudes, standardized them, and then averaged the resulting value
> s. The remaining two indicators were excluded from the index due to their lac
> k of temporal overlap. Nevertheless, we examined whether these discarded vari
> ables differed systematically between individuals raising daughters versus so
> ns and found no significant differences. For the empirical analysis, we estim
> ate the effect of having a daughter (versus a son) on the composite gender eq
> uality index using an Ordinary Least Squares (OLS) model. A positive and stat
> istically significant coefficient would suggest that individuals with daughte
> rs express more egalitarian gender attitudes, thereby supporting H1. Finally,
>  it is important to note that our identification strategy relies on the idea 
> that the gender of the firstborn is a random event. Therefore, a simple OLS m
> odel would be sufficient to test the treatment effect. However, there may be 
> systematic differences between the treated group (i.e., having a daughter) an
> d the control group (i.e., having a son). To address this concern, we examine
>  the balance of covariates across groups prior to conducting the regression a
> nalysis.
.         
.         * Recodification of the variable plj0620. Because higher values of pl
> j0624 and plj0625 indicate more gender-equal attitudes, but not for plj0620, 
> we codify plj0620 for consistency.
.         
.         gen plj0620_v1 = 10 - plj0620 
(30,138 missing values generated)

.                 
.         recode plj0620_v1 (18 = -8) if plj0620 == -8 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for the category: Question not part of the survey this year*/
(3,452 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         recode plj0620_v1 (15 = -5) if plj0620 == -5 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for the category Not included in this version of the survey*/
(1,253 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         recode plj0620_v1 (11 = -1) if plj0620 == -1 /*This restores the orig
> inal category for No answer*/
(26 changes made to plj0620_v1)

.         
.         label variable plj0620_v1 "High values mean equality rights not belon
> ging to democracy"

.         
.         * Standardzation
.         foreach x in plj0620_v1 plj0624 plj0625 {
  2.                 
.                 gen `x'_num = (1)*`x' /*To express the variable as a continuo
> us variable*/
  3.                 label variable `x'_num "Numerical versions of `x'"
  4.                 
.                 egen mean_`x'= mean(`x'_num) if `x'_num >= 0 /*It is observed
>  that each variable appears once. Thus, we have a cross-section design*/
  5.                 egen sd_`x' = sd(`x'_num) if `x'_num >= 0
  6.                 
.                 gen `x'_std = (`x'_num - mean_`x')/sd_`x' 
  7.                 label variable `x'_std "Standarized values of `x'"
  8.         }
